<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  M18 (accounting round). Three defects the corpus-wide accounting gate found,
  each pinned here at the smallest size that shows it.

  1. AN ENTITY'S REPLACEMENT TEXT IS ITS OWN DOCUMENT.
     `<!ENTITY laugh1 "&laugh0;">` makes a claim — laugh1 cannot be expanded
     without laugh0 — and the claim was dropped, because an EntityRef was only
     collected under an OWNING ELEMENT and replacement text has no element above
     it. 611 of the 651-site shortfall sat here; libxml2's
     test/recurse/lol_ig_content.xml is the extreme at 147 sites and 1 edge.
     The owner is the DECLARING ENTITY: laugh1 uses laugh0.

  2. THE FIVE PREDEFINED ENTITIES ARE BUILTIN.
     &lt; &gt; &amp; &apos; &quot; are declared by the specification itself, in
     every document. They arrived as EntityRef nodes and took the only other
     answer, so 4,575 sites across 180 files were stamped
     `resolvable: "unresolved"` with `danglingEntityReference: true` — the
     analyzer asserting a well-formed document references an entity nothing
     defines. &nbsp; is here too, and is NOT predefined: an XML document that
     uses it without declaring it really does reference something undefined, and
     that must stay unresolved. The contrast is the point; a gate that cannot
     tell those two apart is not measuring anything.

  3. NO LENGTH CAP ON A NAME THAT CAME FROM THE FILE.
     libxml2's result/bigentname.xml names an entity 3,965 characters long. Its
     declaration was published (that path has no cap) and both its uses were
     dropped (that path capped at 256), so the graph carried the symbol and none
     of the uses. A cap cannot bound what the file size has not already bounded,
     and a guard that disagrees with the guard on the other side of the same
     fact is a defect rather than a defence.

  A parameter entity is included because the first version of the fix guarded the
  replacement-text walk with `!isParameter` to avoid conflating an EntityRef with
  a PEReference, and that guard was over-broad: t8a.xml's
  `<!ENTITY % deftest '&lt;!ELEMENT test (#PCDATA) >'>` contains a predefined
  reference the document genuinely makes.
-->
<!DOCTYPE catalog [
  <!ELEMENT catalog (title, body)>
  <!ELEMENT title (#PCDATA)>
  <!ELEMENT body (#PCDATA)>
  <!ENTITY greeting "hello">
  <!ENTITY shout "&greeting; &greeting; THERE">
  <!ENTITY % shared "TITLE">
  <!ENTITY bare "&undefinedEntity;">
  <!NOTATION png SYSTEM "image/png">
  <!ENTITY logo SYSTEM "logo.png" NDATA png>
]>
<catalog>
  <title>&greeting; &shout; &lt;tag&gt; &amp; &quot;quoted&quot; &apos;q&apos;</title>
  <body>&nbsp; &bare; %shared;</body>
</catalog>
